# A disk on an AHCI (SATA) controller, as real PCs have: BeeFS mounts from
# it, and files and settings survive a reboot (drivers todo, item D1).
@disk
@sata
$ lspci
~ SATA controller
$ about
filesystem: BeeFS on ATA disk
$ cat /system/boot_count
1
$ mkdir /home/bee
$ echo "kept on a SATA disk" > /home/bee/note.txt
$ cp /home/bee/note.txt /home/bee/copy.txt
$ echo "second line" >> /home/bee/copy.txt
$ layout us
keyboard layout: English (US) (saved)
$ df
~ ^disk: \d+ KiB used, \d+ KiB free, 4096 KiB total
@reboot
~ ^\[disk\] SATA port 0 "QEMU HARDDISK", 4 MiB: BeeFS mounted \(boot #2\)$
$ cat /system/boot_count
2
$ cat /system/boot.log
boot #1
boot #2
$ cat /home/bee/note.txt
kept on a SATA disk
$ cat /home/bee/copy.txt
kept on a SATA disk
second line
$ layout
~ ^ \* us\s+English \(US\)
# The event log was written across both boots.
$ log 50
~ boot
$ rm /home/bee/copy.txt
$ ls /home/bee
~ note\.txt$
!~ copy
@reboot
$ ls /home/bee
~ note\.txt$
!~ copy
# A crash report is saved on the SATA disk too, and shown at the next boot.
$! crash panic
~ \[crash\] the previous boot crashed \(b\d+, panic\): .*deliberate kernel panic
$ cat /system/crash/history.jsonl
~ "kind":"panic","build":"\d+","cmd":"crash","msg":".*deliberate kernel panic
